



<exercise>
<introduction>
<p>
Complete the square to convert each quadratic function from standard form to vertex form. Then identify the vertex.
</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=x^2+8x</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Here, <m>b=8</m> so <m>\frac{b}{2}=4</m> and <m>\pbr{\frac{b}{2}} ^2 = 16</m>. Adding and subtracting this, we get
<md>
y = x^2+8x+16-16
</md> which factors to
<md>
y = (x+4)^2-16.
</md> The vertex is <m>(-4,-16)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=4x^2-12x</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First factor out the <m>4</m> to get <m>y=4(x^2-3x)</m>. Now concentrate only on the <m>x^2-3x</m> quadratic. Here, <m>b=-3</m> so <m>\frac{b}{2}=-\frac{3}{2}</m> and <m>\pbr{\frac{b}{2}} ^2 = \frac{9}{4}</m>. Thus
<md>
x^2-3x \quad=\quad x^2-3x + \frac{9}{4} - \frac{9}{4} \quad=\quad \pbr{x-\frac{3}{2}} ^2-\frac{9}{4}.
</md> Multiplying back by the <m>4</m> we factored out originally,
<md>
y \quad=\quad 4\pbr{\pbr{x-\frac{3}{2}} ^2 - \frac{9}{4}} \quad=\quad 4\pbr{x-\frac{3}{2}} ^2-9.
</md> The vertex is <m>\pbr{\frac{3}{2}, -9} </m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=x^2-2x-15</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Here <m>b=-2</m> so <m>\frac{b}{2}=-1</m> and <m>\pbr{\frac{b}{2}} ^2=1</m>. Adding and subtracting this,
<md>
y \quad=\quad x^2-2x+1-15-1 \quad=\quad (x-1)^2-15-1 \quad=\quad (x-1)^2-16.
</md> The vertex is <m>(1,-16)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=3x^2-20x+7</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First factor out the <m>3</m> from the first two terms to get <m>y=3(x^2-\frac{20}{3}x) + 7</m>. Now, concentrate only on the <m>x^2-\frac{20}{3}x</m> quadratic. Here, <m>b=-\frac{20}{3}</m> so <m>\frac{b}{2}=-\frac{10}{3}</m> and <m>(\frac{b}{2})^2 = \frac{100}{9}</m>. Adding and subtracting this,
<md>
x^2-\frac{20}{3}x  \quad=\quad x^2 - \frac{20}{3}x + \frac{100}{9} - \frac{100}{9} \quad=\quad \pbr{x-\frac{10}{3}} ^2 - \frac{100}{9}.
</md> Now, multiply by the <m>3</m> that we factored out initially to get
<md>
y \quad=\quad 3 \pbr{\pbr{x-\frac{10}{3}} ^2 - \frac{100}{9}} + 7 \quad=\quad 3\pbr{x-\frac{10}{3}} ^2 - \frac{100}{3} + 7 \quad=\quad 3\pbr{x-\frac{10}{3}} ^2 - \frac{79}{3}.
</md> The vertex is <m>(\frac{10}{3}, -\frac{79}{3})</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=5x^2-10x+48</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Factor out the <m>5</m> from the first two terms to get <m>y=5(x^2-2x)+48</m>. Now, concentrate only on the <m>x^2-2x</m> quadratic. Here, <m>b=-2</m> and so <m>\frac{b}{2}=-1</m> and <m>\pbr{\frac{b}{2}} ^2=1</m>. Therefore
<md>
x^2-2x \quad = \quad x^2-2x+1-1 \quad = \quad (x-1)^2-1.
</md> Now, multiply by the <m>5</m> that we factored out initially to get
<md>
y \quad = \quad 5\pbr{(x-1)^2-1} + 48 \quad = \quad 5(x-1)^2-5+48 \quad = \quad 5(x-1)^2+43.
</md> The vertex is <m>(1,43)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
<m>y=4x^2-2x+1</m>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Factor out the <m>4</m> from the first two terms to get <m>y=4(x^2-\frac{1}{2}x) + 1</m>. Now, concentrate only on the <m>x^2-\frac{1}{2}x</m> quadratic. Here, <m>b=-\frac{1}{2}</m> so <m>\frac{b}{2}=-\frac{1}{4}</m> and <m>\pbr{\frac{b}{2}} ^2 = \frac{1}{16}</m>. Therefore
<md>
x^2-\frac{1}{2}x \quad = \quad x^2 - \frac{1}{2}x + \frac{1}{16} - \frac{1}{16} \quad = \quad \pbr{x-\frac{1}{4}}^2 - \frac{1}{16} .
</md> Now, multiply by the <m>4</m> that we factored out initially to get
<md>
y \quad = \quad 4 \pbr{\pbr{x-\frac{1}{4}} ^2-\frac{1}{16}} + 1 \quad = \quad 4\pbr{x-\frac{1}{4}} ^2 - \frac{4}{16} + 1 \quad = \quad 4\pbr{x-\frac{1}{4}} ^2 +\frac{3}{4}.
</md> The vertex is <m>\pbr{\frac{1}{4}, \frac{3}{4}} </m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>


</exercise>
